# 5. THREE DIFFICULTY MODES

Every game must offer:

- EASY
- MEDIUM
- HARD

Provide a visually clear difficulty selector before gameplay starts.

Use distinct visual treatments for each option while maintaining accessibility and sufficient contrast.

Each difficulty must make a real gameplay difference.

Do not simply rename the same gameplay three times.

## EASY

Designed for beginners.

Depending on the game, this may include:
- Simpler challenges.
- More forgiving rules.
- More hints.
- Longer time limits.
- Fewer pieces, cards, or distractions.
- More accessible introductory levels.

## MEDIUM

Designed for players who understand the basics.

Depending on the game, this may include:
- More complex challenges.
- Moderate time limits.
- Fewer hints.
- Increased board or puzzle complexity.
- More demanding completion conditions.

## HARD

Designed for experienced players.

Depending on the game, this may include:
- More complex challenges.
- Tighter time limits where appropriate.
- Fewer hints.
- More demanding puzzle configurations.
- Higher completion requirements.

Do not make Hard unfair or impossible.

## Difficulty and levels

The ten levels should form a progression within the selected difficulty mode.

For example, Easy Level 10 can be more challenging than Easy Level 1 without becoming equivalent to the hardest challenge in Hard mode.

Where appropriate, keep the progression state of Easy, Medium, and Hard separate.

Switching difficulty must not silently overwrite progress belonging to another difficulty.

Persist the selected difficulty and its progress when technically appropriate.

